  "dom_id": "step-q343665",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196902/assets/d7aa23647a61b4d13e9468eafc34947e8b01046a31d67da8ee997476e13dec95.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $2\\pi .$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-3\\pi .$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-3\\pi  \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\pi -2\\pi  \\\\  & =-5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3\\pi ,-5\\pi \\}=-3\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-3\\pi ,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$",
